Conceptual Diagram (Flow)
Here's a simple conceptual flow of how the system interacts:

graph TD
    A[Transport Manager] --> B{Buses}
    A --> C{Routes}
    A --> D{Students}

    B --> B1[Bus 1]
    B --> B2[Bus 2]

    C --> C1[Route 1]
    C --> C2[Route 2]

    D --> D1[Student 1]
    D --> D2[Student 2]

    B1 -- assigned_students --> D1
    B1 -- assigned_students --> D2

    C1 -- assigned_buses --> B1

    A -- "add/view/manage" --> B
    A -- "add/view/manage" --> C
    A -- "add/view/manage" --> D

    A -- "assign_student_to_bus" --> D
    A -- "assign_student_to_bus" --> B

    D -- "pay_fees" --> A
Explanation of the Diagram:

Transport Manager: The central entity that orchestrates all operations.
Buses, Routes, Students: These are the core data entities managed by the Transport Manager.
Relationships:
Students are assigned to Buses: Each student can be linked to a specific bus.
Buses are assigned to Routes: Each route can have one or more buses serving it.
Operations: The Transport Manager provides functions to:
Add, view, and manage Buses, Routes, and Students.
Assign students to buses (which also updates bus capacity).
Manage transport fees for students.
This provides a basic, object-oriented structure to handle the requirements. You can further expand this by adding features like persistence (saving data to files/database), more complex route planning, dynamic capacity adjustments, and a user interface.

